.TH SOFTKBD 2
.SH NAME
softkbd \- ask for a keyboard on the screen
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "softkbd.m";
softkbd := load Softkbd Softkbd->PATH;

init:       fn(): string;
show:       fn(mode: int);
set_rect:   fn(x, y, w, h: int);
clear_rect: fn();
mode:       fn(prose: int);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
A program whose text field takes the keyboard, on a screen with no
keyboard of the machine's, asks for one on the screen with
.IR softkbd .
.B Show
with
.B SLIDE
(a field at the bottom of the screen: the view may be moved up to keep
it above the keyboard) or
.B KEEPTOP
(a field filling the screen: the top stays) asks for it;
.B HIDE
says no field has the keyboard.
.B Set_rect
says where the field is, in screen pixels, for a keyboard the view must
be moved above;
.B clear_rect
takes that back.
.B Mode
says what the field holds: prose (1), whose words a keyboard may put
right and capitalise, or not (0: a shell's commands, code).
.PP
With InferNode's own keyboard running
.RI ( wm-osk (1),
its files at
.BR /mnt/osk ,
.IR osk (4))
the requests are its:
.BR show ,
.BR hide ,
.BR rect ,
.BR mode .
Otherwise they are
.BR /dev/consctl 's
.B kbd
verbs, for the system's keyboard on iOS and Android
.RI ( cons (3));
elsewhere these do nothing, and
.B mode
is not sent.  The environment variable
.B SOFTKBD_PATH
names another file for them, for a test.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/softkbd.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR wm-osk (1),
.IR osk (4)
